         <title><![CDATA[Quebec's legislative session in limbo as court mandates oath to the King]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/montreal/quebec-lawyer-block-parliamentary-work-oath-king-9.7431849?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/6ca105e8-9d9d-4829-987a-702608fded84,1795459939965/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C201%2C3872%2C2178%29%3B' alt='man coming out of car with coat' width='620' height='349' title='Premier of Quebec, Paul St-Pierre Plamondon, arrives at city hall in Montreal, on Monday, Nov. 23, 2026.'/><p>A Quebec Superior Court judge has suspended the province’s law that made the oath to King Charles optional, ordering that members of the province's legislature must swear allegiance before sitting at the National Assembly. Premier Paul St-Pierre Plamondon said Monday he would refuse to take the oath, and that he would fight the ruling.</p>]]></description>
