         <title><![CDATA[More Canadians are using cannabis daily than are drinking alcohol daily: study]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/health/daily-cannabis-use-higher-alcohol-cigarettes-9.7430174?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/97c8ae9e-a2bd-4e78-a6fa-374c73fc130f,1795199612799/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C425%2C6000%2C3375%29%3B' alt='A hand holds a lit joint.' width='620' height='349' title='A man holds a joint while smoking marijuana, in Vancouver on Wednesday Oct. 17, 2018. British Columbia has released the results of its public engagement to help inform the government&apos;s decision on allowing spaces for consuming cannabis.'/><p>Daily use of cannabis has outstripped daily use of alcohol in Canada, with young Canadians at the front of the trend, new data shows. Experts say this generational shift brings some positives, but that many Canadians remain unaware that there are risks with overuse of cannabis.</p>]]></description>
